<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pulakeshin II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 610–<a href="/years/642">642</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Pulakesi II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pulakeshin II was the greatest king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanauj</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>, conquered the Vishnukundinas and installed his brother in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a>. His victories are recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> inscription of 634. He was killed when the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a> captured Badami in 642.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii.jpg" alt="Pulakeshin II" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulakeshin_statue,_badami.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
